    "n-8tCxFy": { "data": { "text": "", "type": "h2" }, "type": "text" },
    "n-8tntjB": {
      "config": { "size": "wide" },
      "data": {
        "text": "<strong>Khafre’s pyramid is the second major royal pyramid at Giza. It can appear taller than Khufu’s because it stands on higher ground, although it is actually smaller. A small cap of original limestone casing remains near its peak. Its complex links the pyramid with a mortuary temple, causeway and valley temple.</strong>",
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      "type": "text"
    },
    "n-AS5ciu": {
      "config": { "size": "wide" },
      "data": { "text": "Pyramid of Kufu", "type": "h3" },
      "type": "text"
    },
    "n-Av3cUJ": {
      "data": { "image": "r-CPdEFP" },
      "config": { "resource": "r-2CCqtd" },
      "type": "image"
    },
    "n-Ay0QZ3": { "children": ["n-DZEfn7"], "type": "carousel" },
    "n-c7krG1": {
      "config": { "size": "wide" },
      "data": {
        "text": "<strong>The Great Sphinx combines the body of a lion with a human head wearing royal headdress. It was carved directly from limestone bedrock near Khafre’s valley temple. Most archaeological evidence points to Khafre’s reign, though aspects of its history remain debated. The monument connects kingship with strength, guardianship and sacred landscape.</strong>",
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      "type": "text"
    },
