    "n-FXkkTg": {
      "config": { "size": "wide" },
      "data": {
        "text": "<strong>Menkaure’s pyramid is the smallest of the three principal Giza pyramids. Its surviving lower courses include red granite casing, while upper portions were faced with limestone. Nearby subsidiary pyramids and temples show that the site was a complex ceremonial landscape, not simply three isolated stone monuments.</strong>",
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      "type": "text"
    },
    "n-JhpXt8": { "data": { "attribution": "", "content": "" }, "type": "attribution" },
    "n-KQ2Hkd": {
      "config": { "size": "wide" },
      "data": {
        "text": "<strong>The Great Pyramid was built for Pharaoh Khufu. Originally about 146.6 meters tall, it is the largest pyramid at Giza and the only largely surviving Wonder of the Ancient World. Millions of limestone blocks form its immense mass, originally covered in smooth white casing stones. The pyramid was the central part of a larger royal funerary complex.</strong>",
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      "type": "text"
    },
